- **D7 THE SERIAL.** Accepted: 1 to 64 characters of `A` to `Z`, `a` to `z`, `0` to `9` and `-`, nothing else (no space anywhere, no trimming). Refused: the literal `NoSerial` in any case (pointer 21). The KEY is the serial upper-cased by `ToUpperInvariant`; the reported form is kept beside it. The key is the primary key of the device table, so a second device whose serial differs only by case is refused by the registry AND by the database. Alternative: ordinal without normalising. Cells: C01, C02, C12.
- **D8 ADDRESS AND DNS NAME, TWO FIELDS, BOTH OPTIONAL.** Address: an IPv4 address of four decimal parts without leading zeros, or an IPv6 address, accepted ONLY if `IPAddress.Parse(text).ToString()` equals the text after IPv6 is lower-cased (so `1234`, `0x7f.0.0.1`, `010.000.000.001`, a port, a scheme and a zone id `%eth0` are refused; `2001:DB8::1` is stored as `2001:db8::1`). DNS name: lower-cased; labels of 1 to 63 of `a-z0-9-`, not starting or ending with `-`; total at most 253; no trailing dot; refused if it parses as an IP address; non-ASCII refused. Alternative: one free-text host field (`A5:206` is the shape not to copy). Cells: C03, C04.
- **D9 PLATFORM.** Closed: `oxpd2`, `oxpd1` (pointer 4), parsed exactly (lower case). Any other stored text read back is a typed error (fail closed). Cells: C05, C24.
- **D10 PANEL CLASS AND REGISTRATION STATE: CLOSED LISTS, NO TRANSITION RULE.** Panel class: `unknown`, `large`, `medium`, `small`, code names for the three classes `02:148` lists (DERIVED from that line). Which class a panel between 4.3 and 8 inches takes is not said there (NOT DECIDED item 4). Registration state: `discovered`, `onboarding`, `registered`, `failed`, `retired`. **No transition between states is enforced in this lane** (WP1.11 and WP2.2 own transitions). Cells: C05, C24.
- **D11 HEALTH.** `unknown`, `ok`, `degraded`, `unreachable`, plus last-seen time, plus the last clock check's result (D18). No free-text error. Cells: C05, C26.
- **D12 CREDENTIAL REFERENCES, NAMES ONLY.** A device holds zero or one reference of each kind of a closed list: `oxpd1-admin-password`, `oxpd2-solution-refresh-token`, `callback-credential` (pointer 13). A reference is a NAME that follows the secret store's name rule, re-expressed here as text, never referenced as code: 1 to 200 characters of `a`-`z`, `0`-`9`, `.`, `_`, `-`, `/`, starting with a letter or a digit, no `..`, no empty segment (MEASURED: the rule's words at `main` `61b17ec0`, `src/Datasec.Suite.Secrets/SecretNames.cs:7-8`). **No kind, column or property exists for an OXPd 2 admin password** (OQ-2). Named limit: a value that happens to follow the name rule cannot be told from a name. Cell: C06.
- **D13 CAPABILITY SNAPSHOT.** A JSON OBJECT as UTF-8 text, at most 262,144 bytes, parsed with the default maximum depth of `System.Text.Json` (DERIVED: 64; UNMEASURED in 10.0.15) before it is accepted; every snapshot is a new row with its time and the firmware at that time; the registry returns the newest. No pruning. Cells: C09, C17.
- **D14 DEFECT-TABLE MATCH.** Device key, defect id (1 to 64 of `a-z0-9.-`), the firmware it matched, its time. No catalogue. Cell: C10.
- **D15 THE AUDIT LOG IS APPEND-ONLY IN THE LIBRARY.** Fields, closed: id (a database-generated long), time (UTC), actor kind (`system`, `admin`, `device`, `user`), actor id (opaque, 1 to 200 printable ASCII without space; never card data), action (`card-lookup`, `sign-in`, `token-redemption`, `print-release`, `admin-action`, `device-registry`), outcome (`success`, `refused`, `failed`), device key (optional). **No free-text field.** `SaveChanges` refuses any audit entry in state Modified or Deleted with a typed exception and saves nothing. **Named limits, each with a cell that ASSERTS the limit:** `ExecuteUpdate`, `ExecuteDelete` and raw SQL bypass the change tracker; there is no grant or trigger in either database. Cells: C15, C16.
- **D16 STALE UPDATES.** The device row carries an integer `Version`, a concurrency token, raised by one by every registry update; an update from a stale read is a typed conflict and saves nothing. Cell: C13.
- **D17 TIME.** Every clock read goes through `System.TimeProvider` (the tests subclass it). Every stored time is UTC: the model maps `DateTimeOffset` to a UTC `DateTime` column by a value converter and reads it back with offset zero (why, DERIVED, UNMEASURED: Npgsql refuses a non-zero offset for `timestamp with time zone`; SQLite cannot order a `DateTimeOffset` in a query). Cells: C14, C07, C08.
- **D18 THE CLOCK CHECK.** `IReferenceClock` (a reference UTC time, or none) and `IDeviceClockReader` (a device's clock by serial key, or none) are interfaces only. `ClockCheck` compares the server's `TimeProvider` with the reference (the SERVER check) and a device's clock with the server's (the DEVICE check), returning `within`, `outside` or `unknown` with the offset in whole seconds. Tolerance: a setting, **default 300 seconds, inclusive**; `unknown` when a source returns none or throws; `unknown` is never `within`. Alternative tolerance: 3,600 seconds (pointer 19). Cells: C07, C08, C26.
- **D19 DELETE.** `Remove(serial)` deletes the device row and, by cascade, its snapshots and matches; audit rows keep the device key as text with no foreign key and survive. Cell: C17.
- **D20 NAMES IN THE DATABASE.** EF Core's default names; the default schema of each provider; the history table `__EFMigrationsHistory`. Cell: C21.
- **D21 THE REGISTRY CONTRACT** (names are defaults): `IDeviceRegistry` with `Add`, `Find(serial)`, `List()`, `Update(device, expectedVersion)`, `Remove(serial)`, `RecordCapabilities`, `RecordDefectMatch`, `RecordClockCheck`. `IAuditLog.Append`; `AddSuiteData(options)` (provider `PostgreSql` or `SqlServer`, a connection string supplied by the caller) for a later host; **you do not touch the host**; the options type's `ToString()` never holds the connection string (C23). One typed exception per library with a reason code.
