## THE CONTRACT (by PROPERTY first; the names are defaults)
**The property.** Every value the device model holds passed a closed rule before it is held; it holds references to secrets and never a secret; a clock comparison never reports `within` when it does not know; the registry holds exactly one record per printer keyed by a normalised serial; every time it stores is UTC; a stale update saves nothing; the audit log only grows through the library; one model describes the database and each provider's migration set produces it, which a committed script per provider makes visible; nothing it throws or prints holds a connection string. **At least these parts (any other shape: prove it meets the property and every control, or STOP):**
- In Devices: `Device` (the fields of `02:361` with the types of D7 to D14), `SerialNumber` (parse, key), `DeviceAddress`, `DeviceDnsName`, `Platform`, `PanelClass`, `RegistrationState`, `HealthStatus`, `CredentialReference` and `CredentialKind`, `CapabilitySnapshot`, `DefectMatch`, `IDeviceRegistry`, `IReferenceClock`, `IDeviceClockReader`, `ClockCheck`, `ClockCheckResult`, `ClockCheckOptions`, `DeviceRuleException` with a reason code.
- In Data: `SuiteDbContext`, `PostgreSqlSuiteDbContext`, `SqlServerSuiteDbContext`, the two migration sets, `EfDeviceRegistry`, `AuditEntry` and `IAuditLog`, `SuiteDataOptions`, `AddSuiteData`, `SuiteDataException` with a reason code (at least: duplicate device, stale update, audit entry changed, unknown stored value).
- `src/Datasec.Suite.Devices/README.md` and `src/Datasec.Suite.Data/README.md`: what each holds; the rules of D7 to D14; a section `## Limits` that says plainly at least: the reference clock and the device clock read are interfaces only; a credential-reference value that follows the name rule cannot be told from a name; no run against a real PostgreSQL or SQL Server yet; the SQLite schema comes from the model; the audit log is append-only in the library only (D15's limits); no sessions, jobs or Universal Print tables; how to regenerate migrations and snapshots with the scratch program of D5 (described, not committed); that the packages came into the cache by one recorded fetch and every later restore is offline (no reviewer's or seat's name, no B-number).
**Code rules the build enforces (DERIVED from `Directory.Build.props` at `main`, lines 4 and 6: nullable on, warnings are errors):** no `#pragma warning disable` and no `NoWarn` in any file of yours, EXCEPT inside the scaffolded migration files exactly as the scaffolder writes them (name every such line in FOR TUESDAY); no `Console` output, no logger, no telemetry; no `unsafe`; no reflection into EF Core's internal types in committed code; no `System.Net.Http`, no socket, no file I/O in either library.

## THE SHAPES NOT TESTED (the required section: for every property that says every, all, none or no, what the subject can be handed that is NOT the shape a naive test uses; each with a cell or a named limit)
| Property | What it may be handed | Cell or named limit |
|---|---|---|
| Every printer has exactly ONE key | a serial differing only by case (`SYNTH0001`, `synth0001`); the same under the current culture `tr-TR` (`synthi001` must key to `SYNTHI001`); a leading or trailing space; a full-width or other non-ASCII lookalike; `NoSerial` in any case; 65 characters; a duplicate inserted through the context, bypassing the registry | C01, C02, C12 |
| The address holds ONLY an IP address | a DNS name (`printer01.example.test`); `1234` and `0x7f.0.0.1` (which a plain parse accepts: DERIVED, UNMEASURED); leading zeros; `:443`; `https://`; a zone id `%eth0`; upper-case IPv6 | C03 |
| The DNS name holds ONLY a name | an IP literal; a 64-character label; 254 characters; an underscore; a leading `-`; an empty label; a trailing dot; non-ASCII | C04 |
| NO credential value is held | a value with upper case, a space or `=`; 201 characters; a kind string for an OXPd 2 admin password; a value that follows the name rule | C06; the last is a NAMED LIMIT (D12) |
| A clock check is NEVER `within` without knowing | a server clock in 1970; a device clock in 2099; a reader that throws; a reference that answers none; an offset of exactly the tolerance; one second past it, both signs | C07, C08 |
| EVERY snapshot is a JSON object within bounds | not JSON; a JSON array; 262,145 bytes; nesting deeper than the default maximum; an empty object (accepted) | C09 |
| EVERY defect id follows its rule | 65 characters; an upper-case id; an empty id | C10 |
| Every migration applies ONCE | the script run twice on a database that already has it; the two sets drifting from the model; a snapshot compared on a CR LF checkout; the two scripts creating different tables | C20 (each migration id inside a history guard, by text), C19, C21; **the run twice against a real database: NAMED LIMIT, UNMEASURED, a CI change** |
| EVERY stored time is UTC | a `+11:00` value; a time read back after a new context | C14 |
| The audit log NEVER changes | a tracked entry modified; removed; a detached entry attached as Modified; `ExecuteDelete`; `ExecuteUpdate`; raw SQL | C15; the last three: NAMED LIMITS, each ASSERTED by a cell of C15 |
| NO update is lost | two contexts saving the same device; an update with an old `Version` | C13 |
| NO stored value is read as a default | a platform, state, health or kind text in a row the code does not know (written by raw SQL) | C24 |
| NOTHING prints a connection string | the options' `ToString()`; the message of every typed exception | C23 |
